# Example Azure Kubernetes Service load balancer configuration

This example shows how to configure an example LoadBalancer service into Azure AKS.

## Prerequisites

* You have created an AKS cluster and added it to your Azure subscription. If you haven't, see [AKS quickstart](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/quick-start).
* In order for this example to run successfully, you must be able to access the proxy through port forwarding or SSH tunneling.

```
$ az aks browse --resource-group my-resource-group --name my-cluster
```

You should end up with something like:

```sh
$ curl localhost:30080
Welcome to the AKS load balancer!
Use CTRL+C to exit
```

The default `kubectl` context will now switch to the load-balancer service you've configured.

## Building the image

To build the Docker image, follow these steps:

1. Clone this repository:
    ```sh
    $ git clone https://github.com/Azure-Samples/application-gateway-kubernetes-ingress.git
    ```

2. Change directory to the project root folder:
    ```sh
    $ cd application-gateway-kubernetes-ingress
    ```

3. Build the docker images:
    ```sh
    $ make build
    ```
    
    The resulting Docker images can be found under `build/images`.

## Deploying the service

Next, deploy the load-balanced service using the following command:
    
    $ kubectl apply -f examples/kubernetes-deployment.yaml
    
Note that since the ingress controller uses [Portainer for Docker](https://www.portainer.io/) for managing containers, its services are not exposed over load balancers yet.

After some time, your services should be accessible via the [Portainer for Docker dashboard](https://localhost:9000) on port 9000.

